## What it does

`shouldComponentUpdate` is a class-component lifecycle method that returns a boolean. React calls it before re-rendering; return `false` and React skips rendering that component and its subtree for that update.

```jsx
class Row extends React.Component {
  shouldComponentUpdate(nextProps) {
    return nextProps.item !== this.props.item;
  }
  render() { return <li>{this.props.item.label}</li>; }
}
```

## Why it exists

By default a component re-renders whenever its parent does, even if its own props did not change. For most components that is fine — rendering is cheap. But for a component that renders often with identical inputs, or sits over a large subtree, the wasted work adds up. `shouldComponentUpdate` is the manual opt-out.

## PureComponent

Writing the comparison by hand is error-prone, so `React.PureComponent` implements `shouldComponentUpdate` for you as a shallow compare of props and state.

## The function-component equivalent

`React.memo` is the modern counterpart — it wraps a function component and shallow-compares props. Same idea, same caveat: a shallow compare, so mutating an object or passing a new inline object or function as a prop defeats it. Measure before reaching for it; needless memoisation is its own cost.

## What PropTypes is

PropTypes is a runtime type-check for component props. You declare the props a component expects, and in development React warns in the console when something is missing or the wrong type.

```jsx
import PropTypes from 'prop-types';

function Badge({ count, label }) {
  return <span>{label}: {count}</span>;
}

Badge.propTypes = {
  count: PropTypes.number.isRequired,
  label: PropTypes.string,
};
```

## How it behaves

The check runs at runtime, and only in development builds — production strips it for performance. So it catches a wrong prop when that component actually renders, by logging a warning. It never blocks the build.

## PropTypes vs TypeScript

Before TypeScript was widespread, PropTypes was the standard way to document and check props. TypeScript does the same job better: it checks at compile time, before the code runs, covers far more than props, and powers editor autocomplete.

```tsx
type BadgeProps = { count: number; label?: string };
function Badge({ count, label }: BadgeProps) { /* … */ }
```

## Today

New React code uses TypeScript prop types, not PropTypes. You still see PropTypes in older JavaScript codebases and some libraries — worth recognising, not worth adding to a new TS project.
